Reflection on Creating a Portfolio Website

As a 3rd year BSIT student, creating my own portfolio website has been one of the most meaningful and practical experiences so far. This project not only allowed me to apply what I’ve learned in web development, but also helped me understand the importance of presenting my work in a professional and organized way. Through this task, I improved my skills in HTML, CSS, and a bit of JavaScript, and became more familiar with how to design a clean, user-friendly interface. I also learned how to structure content effectively—deciding what to include, how to present my past projects, and how to highlight my strengths without overwhelming the visitor.

This project also gave me the push I needed to finally build a personal website portfolio, something I had been planning but never completed until now. As we all know, it’s essential for IT students to compile and present their past projects—not just for academic requirements, but for real-world opportunities. A portfolio serves as proof of our capabilities when applying for internships, freelance work, or future jobs. It’s like a digital resume that speaks louder through real, working examples.

In conclusion, building a portfolio website was more than just a class project—it was a valuable step toward my career as an IT professional. I now feel more prepared to showcase my work to potential clients and employers.
